31
00:02:17,361 --> 00:02:21,753
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

32
00:02:21,754 --> 00:02:24,279
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

33
00:02:27,281 --> 00:02:32,074
Let's make this a workable space for
learning. Cameras are optional when you

34
00:02:32,075 --> 00:02:37,123
join others online. Listening, writing a
private note, speaking, using chat or a

35
00:02:37,124 --> 00:02:41,981
reaction, and choosing to pass are all
valid ways to take part. You can move,

36
00:02:41,982 --> 00:02:46,774
stretch, look away from the screen, or
step away and come back. The original

37
00:02:46,775 --> 00:02:51,760
workshop includes some small breakout
activities. In this recording, I'll offer

38
00:02:51,761 --> 00:02:56,426
quiet individual reflection as the
starting point. If you're watching with

39
00:02:56,427 --> 00:03:01,155
colleagues, you can pause and talk
together if you choose. You don't need a

40
00:03:01,156 --> 00:03:06,269
breakout room to complete the learning.
When you refer to a student, use initials

41
00:03:06,270 --> 00:03:11,382
or a role such as a Year 9 student. Leave
out names, school details and incidents

42
00:03:11,383 --> 00:03:15,600
that could identify someone. Keep personal
reflections private. Any

43
00:03:15,601 --> 00:03:20,266
contribution to a live group's chat should
be something you're comfortable

44
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:25,507
sharing with that group. Take a moment to
settle on one hope for these sessions. It

45
00:03:25,508 --> 00:03:27,232
can stay in your own notes.

46
00:03:30,241 --> 00:03:35,340
Open page four of your Participant Guide,
Think about your students. Bring two or

47
00:03:35,341 --> 00:03:40,504
three students to mind, ideally students
who are quite different from one another.

48
00:03:40,505 --> 00:03:44,967
They might differ in year level,
communication, subjects they enjoy, or

49
00:03:44,968 --> 00:03:49,493
what a school day feels like for them. For
each student, note strengths,

50
00:03:49,494 --> 00:03:54,466
interests or moments of connection you
want to keep visible. Then ask yourself:

51
00:03:54,467 --> 00:03:59,630
What am I here for? What would I like to
understand better? A question is a useful

52
00:03:59,631 --> 00:04:04,666
starting point. You don't have to explain
the student or solve anything yet. Use

53
00:04:04,667 --> 00:04:09,702
Student A, B and C, or initials that only
mean something to you. This reflection

54
00:04:09,703 --> 00:04:14,611
stays private. We'll return to the same
students through the four sessions, so

55
00:04:14,612 --> 00:04:19,520
keep these notes somewhere you can find
them again. Pause here if you'd like a

56
00:04:19,521 --> 00:04:20,859
few minutes to write.

57
00:04:28,881 --> 00:04:33,891
As you come back, look at whether you've
kept the whole person in view. Alongside

58
00:04:33,892 --> 00:04:38,402
a concern or a question, is there
something they enjoy, know a great deal

59
00:04:38,403 --> 00:04:43,413
about, or do with real interest? If that
part of your picture is thin, leave room

60
00:04:43,414 --> 00:04:48,048
to find out. These notes are a starting
point for learning, and we can keep

61
00:04:48,049 --> 00:04:48,988
adding to them.

62
00:04:50,521 --> 00:04:55,029
We're going to listen to Autistic young
people in their own words. Give the

63
00:04:55,030 --> 00:05:00,025
speakers your attention without trying to
diagnose, compare them with a student, or

64
00:05:00,026 --> 00:05:04,533
pull out one lesson that stands for
everyone. Each person's account belongs

65
00:05:04,534 --> 00:05:09,103
to them. As you watch, notice what stays
with you and what you would like to

66
00:05:09,104 --> 00:05:13,368
understand more deeply. We'll return to
those questions after the film.

67
00:07:35,761 --> 00:07:40,090
Take a quiet moment with what you've
heard. On page four of your guide,

68
00:07:40,091 --> 00:07:45,101
underneath your student reflection, note
what stayed with you. What did you notice

69
00:07:45,102 --> 00:07:50,050
in the speakers' own accounts? What would
you like to understand more deeply? Try

70
00:07:50,051 --> 00:07:54,875
to keep the observation close to what
someone actually said. We can be quick to

71
00:07:54,876 --> 00:07:59,824
fill in a person's experience from their
expression or behaviour. Here, let their

72
00:07:59,825 --> 00:08:04,650
words lead. If you're with a live online
group, you may share a word or a short

73
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:09,475
phrase in the chat. Passing is welcome.
Pause the video for more thinking time.

74
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:22,151
Keep any questions that are still open.
You don't need to settle on one shared

75
00:08:22,152 --> 00:08:26,034
interpretation of the film. We're building
a broader picture, and

76
00:08:26,035 --> 00:08:30,828
individual accounts may show us different
things. The sensory and thinking ideas

77
00:08:30,829 --> 00:08:35,318
that follow are ways to ask better
questions. In Session Two, we'll connect

78
00:08:35,319 --> 00:08:37,866
them with communication and the school
day.

79
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:44,108
Our starting point is neurodiversity
affirming. People experience and interact

80
00:08:44,109 --> 00:08:48,389
with the world differently. Different ways
of knowing, doing, being and

81
00:08:48,390 --> 00:08:53,403
belonging deserve recognition. Difference
does not make someone a lesser version of

82
00:08:53,404 --> 00:08:57,990
another person. That means we include
strengths, interests and joy alongside

83
00:08:57,991 --> 00:09:02,577
barriers and support needs. Recognising a
student's strengths doesn't make a

84
00:09:02,578 --> 00:09:07,286
difficult day disappear. Someone can bring
deep knowledge, care and creativity

85
00:09:07,287 --> 00:09:11,872
and still need substantial support. Think
of the students on your page. What

86
00:09:11,873 --> 00:09:16,337
becomes visible when you include what they
enjoy and how they connect? How

87
00:09:16,338 --> 00:09:20,984
might that change the language you use
about them? Instead of a label such as

88
00:09:20,985 --> 00:09:25,204
difficult, we can describe what the
environment asks, what the student

89
00:09:25,205 --> 00:09:29,363
communicates, and what support might help.
We can be specific about a

90
00:09:29,364 --> 00:09:32,665
difficulty while respecting the person
experiencing it.

91
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:38,232
The social model gives us an
environment-first lens. We look for

92
00:09:38,233 --> 00:09:43,224
barriers in places, tasks, routines and
expectations, and ask what could change

93
00:09:43,225 --> 00:09:48,025
around the student. Picture a crowded
corridor between periods. There may be

94
00:09:48,026 --> 00:09:52,825
noise, unpredictable movement, people
brushing past, and very little time to

95
00:09:52,826 --> 00:09:58,010
reach the next room. Before deciding that
the student needs to cope better, we can

96
00:09:58,011 --> 00:10:03,067
ask what options exist. Could information
about the route be clearer? Is there a

97
00:10:03,068 --> 00:10:07,739
less crowded route or a different
transition arrangement worth discussing?

98
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:12,476
Those are possibilities to explore with
the student. We can't know from the

99
00:10:12,477 --> 00:10:16,764
corridor alone which one would help.
Include their communication and

100
00:10:16,765 --> 00:10:21,757
experience, try an appropriate change, and
review whether participation becomes

101
00:10:21,758 --> 00:10:26,686
more workable. Changing an environment
won't remove every difficulty. It gives

102
00:10:26,687 --> 00:10:29,694
us something practical and important to
examine.

103
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:35,100
That question about what can change around
a student also connects with

104
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:36,920
what the law asks of schools.

105
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:39,880
There are three parts to the slide.

106
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:43,060
First, the Disability Discrimination Act

107
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:48,300
1992 makes it unlawful to treat a person
unfairly because of disability,

108
00:10:48,300 --> 00:10:49,900
including in education.

109
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:55,350
Second, the Disability Standards for
Education 2005 explain the right

110
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:59,270
of students with disability to take part
in education on the same basis

111
00:10:59,270 --> 00:11:00,300
as other students.

112
00:11:01,140 --> 00:11:04,740
On the same basis means having
opportunities and choices comparable to

113
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:06,700
those available to other students.

114
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:10,600
Keep that idea beside the routine or
environment you've been thinking

115
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:11,080
about.

116
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:14,620
The third part is what this asks of
schools.

117
00:11:14,620 --> 00:11:18,640
Consult the student, or an associate such
as a parent or carer.

118
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:20,600
Make reasonable adjustments.

119
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:23,280
Prevent harassment and victimisation.

120
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:28,020
Every educator has a part in access,
participation and inclusion.

121
00:11:28,820 --> 00:11:31,880
As you listen, you might make a private
note of one connection with

122
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:32,560
your work.

123
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:36,660
There is no need to share details about a
particular student or school.

124
00:11:36,660 --> 00:11:40,860
We will return to reasonable adjustments
and consultation later in the

125
00:11:40,860 --> 00:11:44,400
series, including our partnership work in
Session Four.

126
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:48,970
For detailed or school-specific questions,
speak with your principal or

127
00:11:48,970 --> 00:11:51,040
the relevant system contact.

128
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:55,500
The Australian Government Department of
Education's Disability

129
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:58,940
Standards for Education page is a place to
read more.

130
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:02,860
Keep the link between our
environment-first approach and these

131
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:05,260
obligations in view as we continue.

132
00:12:07,880 --> 00:12:12,920
Autism can be understood as difference and
dynamic disability. Access and

133
00:12:12,921 --> 00:12:18,311
support needs can change with the task,
the environment, relationships and the

134
00:12:18,312 --> 00:12:23,702
capacity available at that moment. In
secondary school, that may happen within

135
00:12:23,703 --> 00:12:29,443
a single day. A student might participate
comfortably in one subject, then find the

136
00:12:29,444 --> 00:12:34,623
next period much harder. The room,
teacher, instructions, pace and previous

137
00:12:34,624 --> 00:12:40,084
demands may all be different. Managing
something yesterday doesn't tell us what

138
00:12:40,085 --> 00:12:45,405
support is needed today. So we keep our
assumptions flexible. We ask what has

139
00:12:45,406 --> 00:12:50,936
changed, what the task requires, and what
the student can tell us. Strengths and

140
00:12:50,937 --> 00:12:56,677
substantial support needs can both belong
in the picture. Support should respond to

141
00:12:56,678 --> 00:13:02,068
the person and the situation, rather than
to a fixed idea of what that student

142
00:13:02,069 --> 00:13:03,188
ought to manage.

143
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:09,544
Many of us learnt about five senses:
touch, vision, taste, hearing and smell.

144
00:13:09,545 --> 00:13:14,241
This workshop extends that picture to
eight. Proprioception is our sense of

145
00:13:14,242 --> 00:13:19,256
where the body is in space. It involves
information from our muscles and joints.

146
00:13:19,257 --> 00:13:24,208
Think about knowing where your feet are as
you move through a busy room, or how

147
00:13:24,209 --> 00:13:28,905
much pressure to use when carrying
equipment. The vestibular sense concerns

148
00:13:28,906 --> 00:13:33,920
balance and movement. Moving on stairs,
changing direction in a game, rocking in

149
00:13:33,921 --> 00:13:38,427
a chair, or being still after movement can
involve this sense. Different

150
00:13:38,428 --> 00:13:43,188
students may seek or avoid different kinds
of movement. Interoception is our

151
00:13:43,189 --> 00:13:47,822
sense of signals from inside the body.
We'll return to it shortly, when we

152
00:13:47,823 --> 00:13:52,203
consider hunger, thirst and feelings.
Across a school day, senses work

153
00:13:52,204 --> 00:13:56,773
together. A science laboratory, the
canteen, a music room and the bus bay

154
00:13:56,774 --> 00:14:01,598
offer quite different experiences. Keep
the question open: what is this place

155
00:14:01,599 --> 00:14:03,883
like for this student, at this time?

156
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:10,251
Sensory experience includes pleasure,
connection and regulation, as well as

157
00:14:10,252 --> 00:14:15,037
discomfort or overload. We need both parts
in view. Notice sensory joy. It

158
00:14:15,038 --> 00:14:20,283
might be a sound, a texture, a movement or
another experience the student values.

159
00:14:20,284 --> 00:14:25,135
Stimming can also be part of pleasure,
regulation and authentic expression.

160
00:14:25,136 --> 00:14:29,856
Harmless stimming does not need to be
discouraged simply because it looks

161
00:14:29,857 --> 00:14:34,642
different. Think about where you see
enjoyment in the students you have in

162
00:14:34,643 --> 00:14:39,887
mind. If you aren't sure what an action
means for someone, that's a reason to ask

163
00:14:39,888 --> 00:14:45,002
in a way they can use. Avoid treating
difference as disruption by default. What

164
00:14:45,003 --> 00:14:49,657
would the student want other people to
understand about that experience?

165
00:14:51,160 --> 00:14:55,785
This slide introduces detail-first
thinking, sometimes described as

166
00:14:55,786 --> 00:15:01,322
bottom-up processing. The image builds a
whole scene from individual details. It

167
00:15:01,323 --> 00:15:06,930
offers a way to think about how someone
may notice specifics and build the bigger

168
00:15:06,931 --> 00:15:11,976
picture from them. That can help us notice
genuine strengths: recognising

169
00:15:11,977 --> 00:15:16,112
patterns, remembering detail,
understanding systems, finding

170
00:15:16,113 --> 00:15:20,808
inconsistencies, or engaging with a
subject very deeply. Honesty and

171
00:15:20,809 --> 00:15:26,415
directness, logical precision and depth of
connection may also be important parts

172
00:15:26,416 --> 00:15:31,672
of a student's experience. These are
possibilities to notice in individuals,

173
00:15:31,673 --> 00:15:36,999
rather than a checklist every Autistic
student must match. A fuller intake of

174
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:42,536
detail can also create load. Something
that seems like background information to

175
00:15:42,537 --> 00:15:47,653
one person may be very noticeable to
another. We can value precision while

176
00:15:47,654 --> 00:15:53,120
considering what the environment asks of
the student. On page five, Strengths I

177
00:15:53,121 --> 00:15:58,377
can see, choose one strength you've
actually observed in each student. Where

178
00:15:58,378 --> 00:16:02,722
or when does it show up? Pause if you'd
like time to record it.

179
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:15,460
As you review those notes, check that the
strength is described in its own right.

180
00:16:15,461 --> 00:16:19,632
It is something present now that an
educator can recognise and build on.

181
00:16:19,633 --> 00:16:24,450
Where you don't yet have an example, keep
that space open for further listening and

182
00:16:24,451 --> 00:16:25,155
observation.

183
00:16:26,680 --> 00:16:31,702
Now consider where attention goes. The
torch and floodlight on the slide show

184
00:16:31,703 --> 00:16:36,394
two ways attention can spread. A
floodlight covers a wider area. A torch

185
00:16:36,395 --> 00:16:41,284
concentrates light more deeply in one
place. Monotropism is a framework for

186
00:16:41,285 --> 00:16:46,175
understanding attention that channels into
fewer things with greater depth.

187
00:16:46,176 --> 00:16:51,131
Developed by Dinah Murray, Mike Lesser and
Wenn Lawson, it helps us consider

188
00:16:51,132 --> 00:16:55,824
deep interests, transitions and the
experience of flow. It is a lens for

189
00:16:55,825 --> 00:17:00,384
inquiry, with individual differences still
in view. Think of a student

190
00:17:00,385 --> 00:17:05,208
absorbed in coding, a design project,
writing, or a topic they love. Their

191
00:17:05,209 --> 00:17:10,363
attention is invested there. When the bell
goes, stopping and moving to another

192
00:17:10,364 --> 00:17:15,518
subject can ask a great deal. A warning, a
clear place to stop, and support for

193
00:17:15,519 --> 00:17:20,408
the transition may make that shift more
manageable. Deep interests can give

194
00:17:20,409 --> 00:17:25,695
access to learning, memory and joy. Full
absorption can be a valuable experience.

195
00:17:25,696 --> 00:17:30,454
Alongside helping students change
activities when needed, we can look for

196
00:17:30,455 --> 00:17:33,691
opportunities to protect that depth of
engagement.

197
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:40,417
Executive functioning gives us language
for the demands within a task. Planning

198
00:17:40,418 --> 00:17:45,502
is working out the steps. Organisation
concerns the materials and information

199
00:17:45,503 --> 00:17:50,654
needed. Time management includes judging
and using the time available. Working

200
00:17:50,655 --> 00:17:55,872
memory helps us hold information while we
use it. Meta-cognition means thinking

201
00:17:55,873 --> 00:18:01,024
about our own thinking. The other areas on
the slide are emotional regulation,

202
00:18:01,025 --> 00:18:06,175
flexibility, attention, task initiation
and persistence. They include managing

203
00:18:06,176 --> 00:18:10,859
feelings, adapting when something changes,
directing attention, getting

204
00:18:10,860 --> 00:18:15,876
started and continuing with a task.
Consider an assessment handed out in the

205
00:18:15,877 --> 00:18:21,095
last period. The subject knowledge is only
part of what it asks. A student also

206
00:18:21,096 --> 00:18:25,912
needs to understand the instructions,
organise materials, decide where to

207
00:18:25,913 --> 00:18:31,264
begin, plan work over time, and remember
how to submit it. These terms are lenses

208
00:18:31,265 --> 00:18:36,215
for inquiry. They don't tell us why a
particular student is struggling. Ask

209
00:18:36,216 --> 00:18:40,831
which demand needs support. Written steps,
a visible first action or a

210
00:18:40,832 --> 00:18:45,448
clearer schedule may be useful
possibilities to investigate, depending

211
00:18:45,449 --> 00:18:47,320
on the student and the task.

212
00:18:48,840 --> 00:18:53,284
Let's return to interoception, our
awareness and understanding of internal

213
00:18:53,285 --> 00:18:58,033
body signals. Those signals can include
temperature, hunger, thirst and needing

214
00:18:58,034 --> 00:19:02,965
the bathroom. They help us make sense of
how we're doing inside. Alexithymia means

215
00:19:02,966 --> 00:19:07,471
difficulty identifying and describing
emotions and feelings. A person might

216
00:19:07,472 --> 00:19:12,160
find it hard to distinguish excitement
from anxiety, or to find words for what

217
00:19:12,161 --> 00:19:16,726
they feel. The questions on the slide
remind us that an outward sign doesn't

218
00:19:16,727 --> 00:19:21,293
always tell us the inner experience.
Crying, for example, does not give us a

219
00:19:21,294 --> 00:19:25,677
complete explanation of someone's
feelings. In school, we might ask how a

220
00:19:25,678 --> 00:19:30,548
student can communicate a need or access a
break when a signal is unclear or hard

221
00:19:30,549 --> 00:19:35,298
to describe. We shouldn't assume that
appearing calm means feeling comfortable,

222
00:19:35,299 --> 00:19:40,047
or that a student can always explain a
feeling on demand. Keep listening, allow

223
00:19:40,048 --> 00:19:42,848
time and stay curious about what we cannot
see.

224
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:46,860
We're going to put that environment-first
lens to work.

225
00:19:46,860 --> 00:19:52,070
On page five of your guide, choose one
familiar routine in a class or

226
00:19:52,070 --> 00:19:53,080
across the school day.

227
00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:57,880
Moving between periods, entering a
practical science lesson, changing

228
00:19:57,880 --> 00:20:01,960
for P E, or attending an assembly could be
useful starting points.

229
00:20:01,960 --> 00:20:06,420
First, name the routine without
identifying a student or school. Then

230
00:20:06,420 --> 00:20:10,660
list the sensory and task demands before
listing any student responses.

231
00:20:10,660 --> 00:20:14,140
What is the environment asking someone to
manage?

232
00:20:15,060 --> 00:20:18,680
Next, add possible options that might
increase access or choice.

233
00:20:18,680 --> 00:20:22,080
Include how a student could communicate
what helps.

234
00:20:22,080 --> 00:20:25,260
Finally, turn each option into a question
to explore.

235
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:29,560
For example, could a clearer transition
arrangement help here,

236
00:20:29,560 --> 00:20:32,840
and how would we find out with the
student? Work privately, or pause and

237
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:36,200
talk it through with a colleague if you're
watching together.

238
00:20:36,200 --> 00:20:40,940
We are reviewing a routine, not trying to
simulate another person's

239
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:42,680
sensory experience.

240
00:20:43,520 --> 00:20:45,220
One grounded question is enough.

241
00:20:45,220 --> 00:20:48,450
The workshop allows ten minutes for this
activity, including two

242
00:20:48,450 --> 00:20:49,860
minutes for the debrief.

243
00:20:49,860 --> 00:20:54,020
Take a minute to choose your routine, then
use around seven minutes to

244
00:20:54,020 --> 00:20:56,540
explore it, privately or with colleagues.

245
00:20:56,540 --> 00:21:00,380
Pause here while you work, then return for
the debrief.

246
00:21:09,160 --> 00:21:14,373
Look back over your notes. Have you
described demands in the environment and

247
00:21:14,374 --> 00:21:20,005
task, or have you slipped into describing
a student as the problem? If you have an

248
00:21:20,006 --> 00:21:25,428
option in mind, whose knowledge will help
you decide whether it fits? Lighting,

249
00:21:25,429 --> 00:21:30,921
sound, movement, proximity and materials
may all matter differently to different

250
00:21:30,922 --> 00:21:36,205
students. An option is still a question
until you explore it in context. What

251
00:21:36,206 --> 00:21:41,210
could educators change, offer or
investigate before increasing demands on

252
00:21:41,211 --> 00:21:45,660
the student? Keep that question with you
as you return to school.

253
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:51,981
Before Session Two, there is a light
noticing task on page six. Choose one

254
00:21:51,982 --> 00:21:57,156
familiar routine you're already part of.
Notice what supports access, connection

255
00:21:57,157 --> 00:22:02,201
or joy for the students you have in mind.
Notice what seems to add demand, and

256
00:22:02,202 --> 00:22:07,441
when. Then write down what you don't know
yet as a question to explore. You don't

257
00:22:07,442 --> 00:22:12,420
need to set up an extra activity or
collect identifying information. Keep the

258
00:22:12,421 --> 00:22:17,661
notes brief and private. If you don't get
a chance to do this, you can still take

259
00:22:17,662 --> 00:22:22,443
part fully in the next session. We'll use
those observations as we explore

260
00:22:22,444 --> 00:22:27,487
communication, connection and how demands
build across a secondary school day,

261
00:22:27,488 --> 00:22:32,728
across subjects and teachers. Keep your
guide handy, and come back to Session Two

262
00:22:32,729 --> 00:22:37,641
when you're ready. Thank you for the
attention and thought you've brought to

263
00:22:37,642 --> 00:22:38,886
this first session.

264
00:22:46,920 --> 00:22:51,323
Welcome back to Secondary Education
Online. This is Session Two,

265
00:22:51,324 --> 00:22:56,357
Communication, Connection and Daily
Capacity. Have your Participant Guide

266
00:22:56,358 --> 00:23:01,809
nearby and bring back the two or three
students you had in mind in Session One.

267
00:23:01,810 --> 00:23:07,262
If you're joining the series here, you can
choose students now. Use initials or

268
00:23:07,263 --> 00:23:12,715
Student A, B and C, and keep identifying
details out of anything you share. The

269
00:23:12,716 --> 00:23:17,818
choices for taking part remain open. Quiet
listening and private notes are

270
00:23:17,819 --> 00:23:23,061
welcome. If you're with a live online
group, its chat can be another option.

271
00:23:23,062 --> 00:23:28,584
Cameras are optional, and you can pause,
move or step away whenever you need to.

272
00:23:28,585 --> 00:23:33,967
Since the last session, what have you
noticed about a student, a class, or one

273
00:23:33,968 --> 00:23:39,700
part of the day? You may have a note from
page six, or simply a question. It's fine

274
00:23:39,701 --> 00:23:45,013
if nothing new has emerged. Take a moment
to reconnect with your own starting

275
00:23:45,014 --> 00:23:45,432
point.

276
00:23:53,440 --> 00:23:59,006
Today we'll explore different ways of
communicating and connecting. Then we'll

277
00:23:59,007 --> 00:24:04,718
map a school day across the timetable,
consider what may be hidden by the effort

278
00:24:04,719 --> 00:24:10,213
to appear fine, and choose one focus to
carry into design. Page seven of your

279
00:24:10,214 --> 00:24:15,853
guide holds the key ideas. What any one
teacher sees in a single period is only

280
00:24:15,854 --> 00:24:17,588
part of a student's day.

281
00:24:19,120 --> 00:24:23,913
Let's begin with an ordinary question.
Since you woke up today, how have you

282
00:24:23,914 --> 00:24:28,578
communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. Perhaps you've

283
00:24:28,579 --> 00:24:33,372
spoken, sent a message, pointed to
something, shown a photo, moved closer or

284
00:24:33,373 --> 00:24:37,974
moved away. You may have used a gesture, a
communication device, a facial

285
00:24:37,975 --> 00:24:42,640
expression, or taken time before
responding. Take a brief moment to notice

286
00:24:42,641 --> 00:24:47,689
the variety in your own morning. You can
keep it to yourself, or add one example

287
00:24:47,690 --> 00:24:49,351
to your live group's chat.

288
00:24:57,360 --> 00:25:01,709
We move between communication methods
throughout the day, often without

289
00:25:01,710 --> 00:25:06,182
noticing. The online setting makes some of
those choices visible: typing,

290
00:25:06,183 --> 00:25:10,408
speaking, reacting, or waiting before
replying. Speech is one part of

291
00:25:10,409 --> 00:25:15,068
communication. In school, we need to
recognise the ways a student is already

292
00:25:15,069 --> 00:25:19,603
communicating and make sure those ways are
available where they need them.

293
00:25:21,120 --> 00:25:26,486
Understanding and expressing are related,
but they don't necessarily look the same.

294
00:25:26,487 --> 00:25:31,657
Understanding concerns information coming
in and how a person makes sense of it.

295
00:25:31,658 --> 00:25:36,893
Expressing concerns messages going out,
through any communication method. Someone

296
00:25:36,894 --> 00:25:41,867
may speak fluently and still need support
to understand rapid instructions or

297
00:25:41,868 --> 00:25:47,038
unfamiliar subject language. Someone else
may understand much more than they can

298
00:25:47,039 --> 00:25:51,947
readily express in speech. How much
someone speaks does not tell us how much

299
00:25:51,948 --> 00:25:55,940
they understand. A A C stands for
augmentative and alternative

300
00:25:55,941 --> 00:26:01,111
communication. It can include signs, a
communication board or book, typing, or a

301
00:26:01,112 --> 00:26:06,347
communication device. Some people use it
alongside speech and some use it instead

302
00:26:06,348 --> 00:26:11,648
of speech. Communication can also change
with the setting, demands and how someone

303
00:26:11,649 --> 00:26:16,688
is feeling. Think privately about your
room and your subject. How can students

304
00:26:16,689 --> 00:26:21,990
receive information, ask a question, make
a choice or show you what they know? Our

305
00:26:21,991 --> 00:26:26,964
role here is to improve access and work
with the student, family and relevant

306
00:26:26,965 --> 00:26:30,826
team. Individual assessment questions
belong with that team.

307
00:26:32,360 --> 00:26:37,375
The next film brings us back to people's
own accounts of communication. As you

308
00:26:37,376 --> 00:26:41,413
watch, notice what supports communication.
Choose one idea that

309
00:26:41,414 --> 00:26:46,755
stands out to you. Let the speakers lead,
and leave room for different experiences.

310
00:32:19,240 --> 00:32:23,645
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? What strengths

311
00:32:23,646 --> 00:32:28,485
did you notice? What did another person do
that helped, and what difference did

312
00:32:28,486 --> 00:32:32,705
the environment or available communication
method make? Take a moment

313
00:32:32,706 --> 00:32:37,235
to hold onto one idea. You can write it
privately or share it in your live

314
00:32:37,236 --> 00:32:40,833
group's chat if you wish. Pause here for
longer reflection.

315
00:32:48,840 --> 00:32:53,515
Keep that idea as something to explore
with the students you have in mind. The

316
00:32:53,516 --> 00:32:57,948
film offers individual accounts, and we
should avoid turning them into one

317
00:32:57,949 --> 00:33:02,746
communication profile for everyone. One
practical access question travels across

318
00:33:02,747 --> 00:33:07,119
the secondary day: is a student's
communication system available in every

319
00:33:07,120 --> 00:33:11,977
period and with every teacher, including
during transitions? It needs to be where

320
00:33:11,978 --> 00:33:13,312
communication happens.

321
00:33:14,840 --> 00:33:20,239
Double empathy invites us to consider
misunderstanding in both directions. When

322
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:25,640
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, understanding each

323
00:33:25,641 --> 00:33:30,417
other can take work from everyone
involved. In a class discussion, for

324
00:33:30,418 --> 00:33:35,610
example, a teacher may expect a quick
spoken answer. A student may need more

325
00:33:35,611 --> 00:33:41,010
processing time, written information, or
another way to respond. If we see only

326
00:33:41,011 --> 00:33:46,202
the missing spoken answer, we may miss the
mismatch between expectations and

327
00:33:46,203 --> 00:33:51,326
access. Ask whose expectations are shaping
the interaction. What could make

328
00:33:51,327 --> 00:33:56,519
connection safer or clearer? Those
questions apply to group work, corridors,

329
00:33:56,520 --> 00:34:01,434
break times, online spaces, and
interactions with different teachers and

330
00:34:01,435 --> 00:34:07,042
peers. We can examine our own part in the
exchange as well as trying to understand

331
00:34:07,043 --> 00:34:11,681
the student's perspective. Connection does
not have to look one way.

332
00:34:13,200 --> 00:34:18,260
Students connect with people in family,
school, community and online settings.

333
00:34:18,261 --> 00:34:23,255
Some enjoy one-to-one conversation. Some
enjoy being alongside another person

334
00:34:23,256 --> 00:34:28,513
quietly. Shared interests, gaming, clubs,
sport or music can offer important ways

335
00:34:28,514 --> 00:34:33,574
to connect. Contact may be frequent or
occasional and still be valued. Culture

336
00:34:33,575 --> 00:34:38,898
and individual preference also shape how
people interact. We shouldn't assume that

337
00:34:38,899 --> 00:34:43,828
the connection we can see at school
represents a young person's whole social

338
00:34:43,829 --> 00:34:48,626
world. For many adolescents, online
communities and shared-interest groups

339
00:34:48,627 --> 00:34:53,884
are meaningful places of belonging. Keep
privacy in mind as you reflect. We don't

340
00:34:53,885 --> 00:34:58,353
need details about a student's online
activity, identity or community

341
00:34:58,354 --> 00:35:03,283
membership. The useful question is how
each student connects, and what makes

342
00:35:03,284 --> 00:35:05,583
that connection accessible to them.

343
00:35:07,120 --> 00:35:12,358
Turn to the Connection discussion on page
eight. Consider the students you have in

344
00:35:12,359 --> 00:35:16,886
mind. Where do they spend recess and
lunch, and who with? What kinds of

345
00:35:16,887 --> 00:35:21,997
connection does each of them enjoy? Which
parts of the day are socially hardest:

346
00:35:21,998 --> 00:35:26,395
perhaps group work, corridors, the canteen
or P E? Then focus on what

347
00:35:26,396 --> 00:35:31,505
teachers could change to make connection
easier without putting a student on the

348
00:35:31,506 --> 00:35:36,033
spot. You can think through these
questions quietly. If you're watching

349
00:35:36,034 --> 00:35:40,755
with colleagues, pause for a brief
conversation if you choose. There is no

350
00:35:40,756 --> 00:35:45,477
need to share private details about any
student. If you don't know where a

351
00:35:45,478 --> 00:35:50,458
student goes or what they enjoy, write
that as a question. Spending time alone

352
00:35:50,459 --> 00:35:55,504
may be a choice someone values. Pause the
video and take a few minutes with the

353
00:35:55,505 --> 00:35:56,020
prompts.

354
00:36:04,040 --> 00:36:08,975
As you return, look at the change you've
considered. Does it offer a choice, or

355
00:36:08,975 --> 00:36:14,036
does it assume a student ought to connect
in a particular way? Possible questions

356
00:36:14,036 --> 00:36:18,718
might concern giving group roles in
advance, offering a choice of partners,

357
00:36:18,718 --> 00:36:23,716
avoiding unexpected public requests to
speak, or making a quiet lunchtime option

358
00:36:23,716 --> 00:36:28,335
available. These are examples to
investigate with a student. We need their

359
00:36:28,335 --> 00:36:33,080
perspective to understand whether an
option feels helpful. Keep one question

360
00:36:33,080 --> 00:36:35,293
that could guide that conversation.

361
00:36:36,801 --> 00:36:41,609
The battery on this slide gives us a
simple way to talk about energy and

362
00:36:41,610 --> 00:36:46,419
capacity. A day can begin with a different
amount of charge. Actions use

363
00:36:46,420 --> 00:36:51,770
energy, and when the battery runs low, it
may show or it may remain hidden. Some

364
00:36:51,771 --> 00:36:56,647
people describe this through spoons. There
is a limited supply, different

365
00:36:56,648 --> 00:37:01,659
actions use some of them, and low spoons
can make further demands harder to

366
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:06,604
manage. Both images are metaphors that can
open a conversation. They don't

367
00:37:06,605 --> 00:37:11,616
measure a student's internal state. The
right support can also restore some

368
00:37:11,617 --> 00:37:16,764
capacity. A valued activity, a workable
break, clearer information or a safer

369
00:37:16,765 --> 00:37:21,709
connection may make a difference. What
helps will vary. As we map the day,

370
00:37:21,710 --> 00:37:25,840
include what gives something back as well
as what uses energy.

371
00:37:27,361 --> 00:37:32,202
Six classrooms, six teachers, one student.
Take a moment to picture one

372
00:37:32,203 --> 00:37:37,875
student's route through the day. How many
rooms, adults, corridor trips and changes

373
00:37:37,876 --> 00:37:42,856
of expectation does it involve? Each
period can bring a different sensory

374
00:37:42,857 --> 00:37:48,113
setting, subject language and way of
organising a task. Each teacher sees one

375
00:37:48,114 --> 00:37:53,301
part of that experience, with limited
information about what happened before

376
00:37:53,302 --> 00:37:58,974
the student arrived. Something helpful in
period one may be unknown in period four.

377
00:37:58,975 --> 00:38:04,162
Sharing what works is something a school
can plan for. It needn't mean every

378
00:38:04,163 --> 00:38:09,627
teacher discovering the same information
separately. Next session we'll consider

379
00:38:09,628 --> 00:38:15,161
supports that travel, including concise
guidance written with the student. First,

380
00:38:15,162 --> 00:38:20,626
let's put more of the day in one place so
we can see what we may otherwise miss.

381
00:38:22,160 --> 00:38:27,226
Open the school-day map on page nine. You
can use a familiar timetable or the

382
00:38:27,226 --> 00:38:32,159
sample Year 9 Thursday on page ten. The
sample is there if you don't know a

383
00:38:32,159 --> 00:38:37,425
student's whole timetable or would prefer
to avoid using real details. Work from

384
00:38:37,425 --> 00:38:42,691
arrival through home group, each period,
breaks and departure. In each row, note

385
00:38:42,691 --> 00:38:47,957
the room, the teacher and what happens.
Then, for each student you have in mind,

386
00:38:47,957 --> 00:38:53,290
use C for something that may cost energy,
L for something that may lighten demand

387
00:38:53,290 --> 00:38:58,490
or give energy back, and a question mark
where you don't know yet. You can mark

388
00:38:58,490 --> 00:39:03,489
both C and L in the same period. The
sample day includes a relief teacher in

389
00:39:03,489 --> 00:39:08,089
science, an unexpected maths quiz,
changing for P E, group work and an

390
00:39:08,089 --> 00:39:13,355
assessment handed out at the end of the
day. Use those events as questions about

391
00:39:13,355 --> 00:39:18,288
demand. We can't assume their meaning for
every student. Map the whole day,

392
00:39:18,288 --> 00:39:23,287
including corridors, lockers, breaks and
the journey out. Work privately, or

393
00:39:23,287 --> 00:39:28,620
compare general patterns with a colleague
if you choose. Keep the student details

394
00:39:28,620 --> 00:39:33,351
to yourself. Pause here for about seven
minutes, or as long as you need.

395
00:39:41,361 --> 00:39:45,751
Let that first map be a working picture.
It doesn't have to be complete. A

396
00:39:45,752 --> 00:39:50,562
question mark is useful because it shows
where student, family or staff knowledge

397
00:39:50,563 --> 00:39:55,193
could add something. You may also have
noticed how much of the day sits beyond

398
00:39:55,194 --> 00:39:59,702
your own lesson. Keep the map open,
because the next step adds detail to it.

399
00:40:01,241 --> 00:40:06,467
Page eleven asks, What costs? What
lightens? Return to the marks you've just

400
00:40:06,468 --> 00:40:11,485
made. For a cost, name the demand or
uncertainty more precisely. It might

401
00:40:11,486 --> 00:40:16,851
involve a change announced only once,
unclear group expectations, or having to

402
00:40:16,852 --> 00:40:22,566
move quickly through a crowded place. For
a lightener, note what may reduce demand,

403
00:40:22,567 --> 00:40:28,002
restore capacity or bring joy. Include
safe connection and interests as well as

404
00:40:28,003 --> 00:40:33,020
formal support. Where the student's
experience is unknown, add a question

405
00:40:33,021 --> 00:40:38,456
instead of an explanation. Something can
be enjoyable or successfully completed

406
00:40:38,457 --> 00:40:44,031
and still take considerable effort. Pause
for a few minutes to add to the map. If

407
00:40:44,032 --> 00:40:49,607
you're with a live group, you may share
one general pattern under costs, lightens

408
00:40:49,608 --> 00:40:52,462
or questions, without identifying anyone.

409
00:41:00,481 --> 00:41:05,363
Now look across the page. What builds up?
Where might there be an opportunity for

410
00:41:05,364 --> 00:41:09,941
support or recovery? The pattern may
differ between the students you have in

411
00:41:09,942 --> 00:41:14,641
mind, and it may change for the same
student across days. Keep the distinction

412
00:41:14,642 --> 00:41:19,585
between what you've observed and what you
still need to ask. That distinction will

413
00:41:19,586 --> 00:41:21,841
help us design a thoughtful response.

414
00:41:23,361 --> 00:41:28,770
The stack on this slide is a visual
reminder of accumulation. The darker bars

415
00:41:28,771 --> 00:41:34,038
represent demand. The lighter bars
represent relief and recovery. We aren't

416
00:41:34,039 --> 00:41:39,020
assigning scores to students or
calculating their energy. A moment that

417
00:41:39,021 --> 00:41:44,786
seems small in isolation may arrive after
many earlier demands. By the same token,

418
00:41:44,787 --> 00:41:50,410
an earlier support or valued experience
may matter later. No single teacher sees

419
00:41:50,411 --> 00:41:55,820
the whole stack. Glance back at your map
and notice where further information

420
00:41:55,821 --> 00:42:00,232
could change your understanding of a
moment in your own lesson.

421
00:42:01,761 --> 00:42:06,803
Masking concerns the effort someone may
make to hide or manage parts of

422
00:42:06,804 --> 00:42:12,638
themselves in order to fit expectations.
Looking only at outward behaviour or task

423
00:42:12,639 --> 00:42:18,473
completion can leave a great deal unseen.
Keep these questions private. What might

424
00:42:18,474 --> 00:42:24,092
a student be working hard to manage or
hide? Where can they feel recognised and

425
00:42:24,093 --> 00:42:29,712
included? What supports belonging while
allowing them to be themselves? What do

426
00:42:29,713 --> 00:42:34,826
they want others to understand? We can't
determine masking from a single

427
00:42:34,827 --> 00:42:40,157
behaviour, and visible quietness doesn't
give us the whole story. Alongside

428
00:42:40,158 --> 00:42:45,632
demands, keep strengths, joy and safe
connection in view. The next few slides

429
00:42:45,633 --> 00:42:51,468
touch on burnout and School Can't. You're
welcome to pause, look away or step away

430
00:42:51,469 --> 00:42:53,771
and return whenever you need to.

431
00:42:55,281 --> 00:43:00,764
The pressure to fit in can be especially
strong in the secondary years. Masking

432
00:43:00,765 --> 00:43:05,896
may increase as belonging with peers
matters more. Working out who you are

433
00:43:05,897 --> 00:43:11,450
while also hiding parts of yourself can
take a great deal of energy. Burnout and

434
00:43:11,451 --> 00:43:17,004
School Can't can build over time. We will
return to partnership with families in

435
00:43:17,005 --> 00:43:21,996
Session Four, including how home and
school may see different parts of a

436
00:43:21,997 --> 00:43:27,198
student's capacity. Here, the point is to
notice the demands without blame.

437
00:43:27,199 --> 00:43:32,682
Adolescence is also a time of growth.
Young people can build self-understanding

438
00:43:32,683 --> 00:43:38,166
and practise communicating what they need
when school feels safe. A student may

439
00:43:38,167 --> 00:43:43,650
need time, preparation and different ways
to express a view. Feeling safe to be

440
00:43:43,651 --> 00:43:49,063
themselves helps make that possible. We
don't need personal stories in a group

441
00:43:49,064 --> 00:43:53,421
chat to consider what a school could do to
support that safety.

442
00:43:54,961 --> 00:43:59,870
Good and chilled in class. It's a familiar
description, and it's worth

443
00:43:59,871 --> 00:44:05,208
looking beneath it. A quiet student may be
using a great deal of effort that

444
00:44:05,209 --> 00:44:10,260
other people don't see. Calm does not
always mean okay. Sometimes it may

445
00:44:10,261 --> 00:44:15,954
involve masking. Think privately about a
student whose needs are easy to overlook

446
00:44:15,955 --> 00:44:21,433
because they rarely interrupt or ask for
help. What do you actually know about

447
00:44:21,434 --> 00:44:26,415
their experience, and what have you
assumed? There is no need to answer

448
00:44:26,416 --> 00:44:31,182
aloud. Hold the question gently as we move
into the next reflection.

449
00:44:34,201 --> 00:44:38,770
On page twelve, consider the three masking
discussion questions. Which

450
00:44:38,771 --> 00:44:44,068
classes, teachers or times of day might
ask the most of a student to appear fine?

451
00:44:44,069 --> 00:44:49,366
Where at school do they seem most able to
be themselves? Who would notice if they

452
00:44:49,367 --> 00:44:54,333
were struggling, and how would that
information reach the right people? Keep

453
00:44:54,334 --> 00:44:58,902
the first two questions private. If you're
with a live group, the last

454
00:44:58,903 --> 00:45:03,869
question can be discussed as a school
process: how information moves between

455
00:45:03,870 --> 00:45:08,637
subject teachers, year level staff,
learning support, wellbeing staff and

456
00:45:08,638 --> 00:45:13,538
home. Avoid naming a student or an
individual staff member. Pause the video

457
00:45:13,539 --> 00:45:15,856
for a couple of minutes to reflect.

458
00:45:23,881 --> 00:45:29,137
The question about who notices is also a
question about how we listen. Information

459
00:45:29,138 --> 00:45:33,746
may come through a student's own
communication, a family conversation or

460
00:45:33,747 --> 00:45:38,938
something a colleague observes. A useful
process allows that information to reach

461
00:45:38,939 --> 00:45:43,611
the people who can respond, with privacy
respected. If your notes contain

462
00:45:43,612 --> 00:45:48,609
uncertainty, keep it as a prompt for
consultation. It doesn't need to become a

463
00:45:48,610 --> 00:45:51,140
claim about someone's inner experience.

464
00:45:52,641 --> 00:45:57,730
Let's choose a clear focus to carry into
Session Three. On page thirteen, complete

465
00:45:57,731 --> 00:46:02,130
Keep, Reduce and Explore. Keep one
practice because it supports access,

466
00:46:02,131 --> 00:46:06,969
connection or joy. Reduce one barrier or
unnecessary demand. Tick whether that

467
00:46:06,970 --> 00:46:11,808
focus is a lesson, an assessment task or a
handover. Explore one question with

468
00:46:11,809 --> 00:46:16,773
students, families or the team. You don't
need to redesign the whole day. Choose

469
00:46:16,774 --> 00:46:21,926
something specific enough to work with in
the next session. Keep the day map nearby

470
00:46:21,927 --> 00:46:26,954
as your evidence, including the parts you
still need to understand. Pause here to

471
00:46:26,955 --> 00:46:28,398
write your three notes.

472
00:46:36,401 --> 00:46:41,829
Check that your Reduce item describes
something in the environment, task or

473
00:46:41,830 --> 00:46:47,699
routine that educators can examine. Make
sure the Keep item protects what already

474
00:46:47,700 --> 00:46:53,201
works. Your question may be just as
important as your proposed change. Bring

475
00:46:53,202 --> 00:46:57,530
all three, and the map behind them, into
